    "summary": "Mixed‑energy Nassau show with strong Bird Song and Space",
    "blurb": "The concert features standout moments like Brown Eyed Women, Bird Song, and a train‑horn‑enhanced Space. Jerry’s soaring solos lift the Garcia originals while the post‑drums segment rescues an otherwise sluggish second set. Wave to the Wind and a limp Box of Rain keep the momentum uneven.",
    "review": "On March 13, 1992 at Nassau Coliseum the Dead delivered a show that swings between brilliance and lethargy. The first set crackles with energy – Garcia’s lyrical leads on Brown Eyed Women and Masterpiece sit beside Weir’s soulful Bird Song, and the band rides a tight groove into a memorable Space where a rain‑soaked train horn pierces the mix. After the dual drum excursion, the post‑drums jam injects fresh life, but the second half never fully recovers; Wave to the Wind trudges along and the encore Box of Rain feels more like an afterthought than a climax. Fans will love the highlighted moments, yet should temper expectations for a uniformly high‑octane night.\n\nAcross the three available recordings the soundboard captures are solid – the Gardner SBD (gd92-03-13.nak700.gardner.9477.sbeok.shnf) offers the most complete and balanced picture despite minor DAT sizzle, while the Miller SBD is praised for clarity though drums can get buried in dense jams. One audience‑based source deems the entire performance dull, underscoring the mixed reception this night received among listeners.",
